\section{Data}{

With \code{data = "long"} (default), the model is fitted once per analyte to the
study samples, with the analyte's processed values in column \code{value} and all
sample metadata (subject, time, outcome, ...) as further columns, e.g.
\code{value ~ time + subject} or \code{outcome ~ value + age}. With \code{data = "wide"}, it
is fitted once to one row per well with one column per analyte (named as
the analytes; use backticks, e.g. \code{outcome ~ `IL-6` + age}). A \code{.} in
the formula then stands for all analytes plus the variables named in the
formula. With \code{term}, the result has one row per term; otherwise one row
(\code{analyte = "all"}).
